 Binding behaviour lives in `.feature` specs and referenced `assets/**`. History lives in git. These notes carry only what the next cycle needs.
 
-## DOM-identity constraint
+## Cycle 3 — Browser acceptance defects
 
-The external browser acceptance suite (Cypress) fails on every toggle interaction because the `render()` function in `js/app.js` destroys and recreates all `<li>` elements on every state change (`todoList.innerHTML = ''` followed by element-by-element reconstruction). This detaches any DOM reference the browser holds.
+Two acceptance-suite failures remain after the DOM-identity fix.
+Both are real product defects visible only in a real browser.
 
-The in-harness suite (happy-dom + Cucumber) does not catch this because its step definitions re-query the DOM fresh after each action, never holding a reference across a state change.
+### Defect BROWSER-1: removeChild during re-entrant blur (editing to empty)
 
-**The fix:** The `render()` function MUST preserve existing DOM nodes when their data has not changed. When toggling a single todo's `completed` state, mutate the existing `<li>` element's class list in place rather than destroying and recreating it. When toggling all, mutate each `<li>` in place. The `innerHTML = ''` pattern is the defect; it must be replaced with in-place DOM mutation for unchanged nodes.
+**Acceptance test:** `should remove the item if an empty text string was entered`
+**Spec:** `features/browser-acceptance-defects.feature:27`
+**Root cause:** When the user clears the edit input and presses Enter, both `blur` and `keydown(Enter)` fire. Both call `finishEditing()` → `destroyTodo()` → `render()`. The first `render()` removes the `<li>` via `removeChild`, which detaches the `<input>`, firing `blur` synchronously mid-orphan-removal-loop. The blur handler re-enters `render()`, removing the same orphan. The original loop's `existingLis[j]` now references a node whose `parentNode` no longer considers it a child → `removeChild` throws `NotFoundError`.
+**Fix指引:**
+1. Guard the orphan-removal loop: only call `removeChild` if `existingLis[j].parentNode === todoList` (or simply check `parentNode` is not null)
+2. Clear `editingId` in `destroyTodo()` to prevent stale editing state
 
-**Routing defect (test 5):** After navigating between filter views and using browser back, the todo list shows empty. The hashchange handler may not be firing correctly in a real browser, or the filter state is not being restored on navigation.
+### Defect BROWSER-2: Filter click handler blocks hash update (back button)
+
+**Acceptance test:** `should respect the back button`
+**Spec:** `features/browser-acceptance-defects.feature:66`
+**Root cause:** The filter link click handler calls `e.preventDefault()`, which stops the default anchor behaviour (updating `window.location.hash`). The handler calls `setFilter()` directly, which updates the filter state but does NOT update the hash. So clicking filter links does not push history entries. When the user presses the browser back button, `history.back()` has nothing to go back to — the hash stays the same, `hashchange` never fires, and the filter does not change.
+**Fix指引:**
+1. Remove `e.preventDefault()` from the filter click handler and let the default anchor behaviour update the hash (the existing `hashchange` listener will call `handleHashChange()` → `setFilter()`)
+2. OR update `window.location.hash` manually in the click handler before calling `setFilter`
+
+### Previous defects (resolved)
+
+- DOM-identity (toggle/destroy) — resolved by in-place DOM mutation in render()
 
 ## Acceptance test failure evidence
 
-The external suite runs Cypress against the build. Five failures, all DOM-identity or routing:
